  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "t04-q05",
  "speechText": "Basically, for long-running work, I prefer to process it in the background. The API creates a job, and a worker processes it from the queue. If there is a temporary failure, I retry only a few times. But the same message can be delivered more than once, so I also make the consumer idempotent. For example, before I process the job, I can check the job ID and its current status to avoid doing the same work twice. I only ACK the message after the work is completed successfully. Another risk is when the database update succeeds but publishing the next message fails. One common solution is the Outbox Pattern. We save the business change and the event in the same database transaction, then publish the event later. So overall, the important points are background processing, limited retry, idempotency, and clear job status.",
